{
  "teamId": 114,
  "year": 2023,
  "narrative": "The 2023 Cleveland Guardians entered the season as a team with genuine playoff aspirations, built on a stout pitching staff, elite closer Emmanuel Clase, and the offensive engine of José Ramírez. But the first week of April told a different story than anyone expected. Clase surrendered a walk-off two-run home run on Opening Weekend, and the bullpen — Karinchak, Herrin — kept hemorrhaging leads in the season's earliest days. Cleveland staggered out of April at 12-14, already in a hole they would spend the rest of the year trying to dig out of.\n\nMay and June offered the most compelling chapter of the Cleveland season, as Josh Naylor emerged as a genuine run-producer and José Ramírez delivered clutch after clutch. Naylor's back-to-back multi-run home runs in mid-May, Ramírez's reviewed-and-upheld center-field shot, and then the iconic line-drive double to center on May 28th that swung nearly 0.8 win probability — these were the moments that carried Cleveland to winning records in both June and July. Will Brennan and Steven Kwan chipped in timely hits, and the offense finally looked like enough to contend. The team played .536 ball from May through July, keeping the wild card dream alive.\n\nAugust is where the Guardians' season died. A 11-17 record masked just how badly the wheels came off, with Clase — typically one of the game's best closers — suddenly the central figure in a series of gut-punch losses. A throwing error, blown saves, and Yordan Alvarez punishing the Cleveland bullpen for a three-run home run in late July set the tone for a brutal stretch. Royce Lewis crushed a grand slam off Xzavion Curry in late August, and the margin for error evaporated entirely.\n\nSeptember brought flashes of dignity — Kole Calhoun's late-summer heroics, a Steven Kwan clutch single, and David Fry stepping up as an unexpected contributor — but the collapse had already been written. Emmanuel Clase, who had been the backbone of so many Cleveland wins in prior seasons, endured one of the most heartbreaking individual stretches of any reliever in 2023, giving up run after run in high-leverage spots that had historically been his domain. A catastrophic Clase blown save on September 22nd, coughing up a two-run double with a -0.654 WPA swing, was the season's final gut-punch. Cleveland finished 76-86, a team that burned bright in the summer and simply ran out of answers when the calendar turned to fall.",
